Binder ioctl passthrough for qemu linux-user.

The Android binder driver accepts 32-bit processes natively:
drivers/android/binder.c registers .compat_ioctl = compat_ptr_ioctl, and
the binder_uapi structs use explicit fixed-width types (binder_uintptr_t
is __u64), so BINDER_* ioctl numbers and layouts are IDENTICAL for 32 and
64-bit callers. qemu-user's do_ioctl whitelist rejects them with
-ENOTTY/-ENOSYS before ever reaching the kernel, which kills every 32-bit
Android process that opens binder (ProcessState aborts).

This patch forwards every ioctl whose type is 'b' straight to the host
kernel with the guest's raw pointer. It is safe because qemu-user shares
the address space with the translated process: the pointer the guest
passed IS a valid host pointer, and the kernel side handles the 32-bit
compat layout itself via compat_ptr_ioctl.

IHU629G-emulator: revives the OEM 32-bit stack (audioserver,
mediaserver, cameraserver, MTK HALs) under qemu-arm-static on hosts
without AArch32.
--- a/linux-user/syscall.c
+++ b/linux-user/syscall.c
@@ -5574,6 +5574,15 @@ static abi_long do_ioctl(int fd, int cmd, abi_long arg)
     int target_size;
     void *argptr;

+    /* Android binder ioctls: type 'b'. Layouts are 32/64-identical
+     * (fixed-width uapi types) and the kernel handles 32-bit callers
+     * via compat_ptr_ioctl. Pass the guest pointer straight through:
+     * qemu-user shares the address space, so it is a valid host pointer. */
+    if (_IOC_TYPE(cmd) == 'b') {
+        return get_errno(safe_ioctl(fd, cmd, (void *)(uintptr_t)arg));
+    }
+
     ie = ioctl_entries;
